# A change to a value is a step that makes it anew: `board.mark_all()` and `board.reset()` change the board they are
# called on, so each takes the board as it was and makes it at its own moment (`T × A → T`, mathematics.md, "Signatures
# say the arrow"); inside `reset`, `self.cells.clear()` and `self.scores.clear()` are such steps on the board's parts.
# `renamed` changes no input: it copies the board (`copy.copy`, a library operation whose result it names) and renames
# the copy. `merged` is called on that copy with the board, and `Board()` in it reads the fields of both. The lists and
# dicts are of no type of the code's: what `with_tag` gives goes into `tally`, what `tally` gives into `counted`, and
# what `counted`, `extended` and `queued` give goes back to `main`.
route board.core.main
step main/Cell()
step main/Board()
step main/Board.mark_all
step main/Board.mark_all/for cell in self.cells
step main/Board.reset
step main/renamed
step main/Board.merged
step main/Board.merged/Board()
step main/with_tag
step main/tally
step main/counted
step main/extended
step main/queued

line entry > main call
line main/Cell() > Cell@main/Cell() value
line Cell@main/Cell() > main/Board() value
line main/Board() > Board·board@main/Board() value
line main/Board.mark_all/for cell in self.cells > Cell·cell@main/Board.mark_all/for cell in self.cells value
line main/Board.mark_all > Board·board@main/Board.mark_all value
line main/Board.reset > Board·board@main/Board.reset value
line main/renamed > Board@main/renamed value
line Board@main/renamed > main/Board.merged/Board() value                  # self, read by Board() in merged
line main/Board.merged/Board() > Board@main/Board.merged/Board() value
line Board@main/Board.merged/Board() > main/Board.merged value             # merged hands back what its body made
line main/Board.merged > Board·other@main/Board.merged value
line Board·other@main/Board.merged > main value                            # main counts the cells of other
line main/with_tag > _@main/with_tag value
line _@main/with_tag > main/tally value
line main/tally > _@main/tally value
line _@main/tally > main/counted value
line main/counted > main back
line main/extended > main back
line main/queued > main back

home Cell@main/Cell() main
home Board·board@main/Board() main
home Board·board@main/Board.mark_all main
home Cell·cell@main/Board.mark_all/for cell in self.cells main/Board.mark_all
home Board·board@main/Board.reset main
home Board@main/renamed main
home Board@main/Board.merged/Board() main/Board.merged
home Board·other@main/Board.merged main
home _@main/with_tag main
home _@main/tally main

side entry left

# A library operation that changes the value it runs on, or whose result the body names, is a step; in Python it is
# none on a part of `self`, on a local list itself, or where the result is named (it is one on a parameter's part,
# `board.cells.clear()`, on the shortcut side)
known #125 step main/Board.reset/clear
known #125 step main/Board.reset/clear#2
known #125 step main/renamed/copy
known #125 step main/extended/append
known #125 step main/queued/list
known #125 line Board·board@main/Board.mark_all > main/Board.reset/clear value
known #125 line main/Board.reset/clear > Board·self@main/Board.reset/clear value
known #125 line Board·self@main/Board.reset/clear > main/Board.reset/clear#2 value
known #125 line main/Board.reset/clear#2 > Board·self@main/Board.reset/clear#2 value
known #125 line Board·self@main/Board.reset/clear#2 > main/Board.reset value
known #125 line Board·board@main/Board.reset > main/renamed/copy value
known #125 line main/renamed/copy > Board·board@main/renamed/copy value
known #125 line Board·board@main/renamed/copy > main/renamed value
known #125 line main/extended/append > list·xs@main/extended/append value
known #125 line list·xs@main/extended/append > main/extended value
known #125 line main/queued/list > list·jobs@main/queued/list value
known #125 line list·jobs@main/queued/list > main/queued value
known #125 home Board·self@main/Board.reset/clear main/Board.reset
known #125 home Board·self@main/Board.reset/clear#2 main/Board.reset
known #125 home Board·board@main/renamed/copy main/renamed
known #125 home list·xs@main/extended/append main/extended
known #125 home list·jobs@main/queued/list main/queued

# The call of `merged` on what `renamed` gives is handed nothing of `board` (#128, the inner call of a chain): its
# `other` holds nothing the call hands
known #128 line Board·board@main/Board.reset > main/Board.merged/Board() value

# A method that changes its receiver hands it on twice: unnamed from its arrow's change, and named; the next step takes
# the second
known #125 extra line Board·board@main/Board.mark_all > main/Board.reset value   # `self.cells.clear()` inside takes it
known #125 extra line Board·board@main/Board.reset > main/renamed value   # `copy.copy(board)` inside takes it

# The own arrow takes a value its body only hands to a step inside it: the Cell built into `[Cell()]` for `Board()`, the
# receiver of `merged` whose fields go into `Board(..)`

# The loop over `self.cells` goes over the board the method runs on, and `cell.marked = True` is the body's own use of
# each cell: the board reaches the loop through `self` (#128); the own arrow takes it instead (#126)
known #128 line Board·board@main/Board() > main/Board.mark_all/for cell in self.cells value
known #101 line Cell·cell@main/Board.mark_all/for cell in self.cells > main/Board.mark_all value
known #126 extra line Board·board@main/Board() > main/Board.mark_all value
